BRICKS#S#DWARFS
I#F#I#SHY#E#E#T
GO#ASA#U#HAT#OR
B#D#SPOTTER#A#A
EVEN#EATEN#BREW
N#NOW#RED#MAM#S
#R#MAT#R#BIT#A#
JANITOR#DENTIST
#G#NET#B#TEL#S#
S#OER#SAT#RED#S
CAFE#POLES#SOAP
R#T#DOLLARS#T#O
ED#TOP#O#OLD#NO
A#I#O#NOD#O#A#N
MANURE#N#STILLS


A1. Irish confetti. ~ BRICKS
A5. Everybody looks down on these fellows. ~ DWARFS
A9. What the flapper decidedly is not. ~ SHY
A10. You've got to step on it to do this. ~ GO
A11. Masculine name. Bib.: a champion of Jehovah against idolaters. ~ ASA
A13. This is an overhead expense. ~ HAT
A14. That same little crossword conjunction. ~ OR
A16. One who locates the spot on which the crime was committed. ~ SPOTTER
A20. There's nothing odd about this. ~ EVEN
A22. Consumed. ~ EATEN
A23. What lots of people started to do when the Prohibition Law was passed. ~ BREW
A24. At the present time. ~ NOW
A26. Nickname for an I. W. W. ~ RED
A27. A small boy's substitute for "madam." ~ MAM
A29. This is only fit to wipe your feet on. ~ MAT
A31. This is just a morsel. ~ BIT
A33. This fellow should make a good cleanup. ~ JANITOR
A34. This fellow usually looks down in the mouth. ~ DENTIST
A35. There's many a poor fish taken in by this. ~ NET
A37. The way to send a Scotchogram. (Abbr.) ~ TEL
A39. Where high-fliers go. (Poet.) ~ OER
A40. What King Arthur did at the Round Table. ~ SAT
A42. That fool-girl complexion. ~ RED
A45. A filling station. ~ CAFE
A46. These hold the wires in Poland. ~ POLES
A48. This gets the dirt. ~ SOAP
A49. What it takes to make a wife happy. (Plural.) ~ DOLLARS
A51. Someone to whom this puzzle was acceptable. (Abbr.) ~ ED
A52. This often goes for a spin. ~ TOP
A53. This kind of person has lived. ~ OLD
A54. This is negative. ~ NO
A56. What a dry sermon makes you do. ~ NOD
A58. This is used—for the land's sake. ~ MANURE
A59. These are always making a kick. (Plural.) ~ STILLS

D1. A good eye-opener. (Double word.) ~ BIGBEN
D2. A little conjunction that always gets in the way. ~ IF
D3. This carries germs they say—but no one seems to care. ~ KISS
D4. This is only a blind. ~ SHUTTER
D6. What Al's brown derby was meant for. ~ WEAR
D7. A note in the musical scale. ~ RE
D8. These are suckers. ~ STRAWS
D12. To mimic. ~ APE
D13. This came out of its shell a long time ago. ~ HEN
D15. This is only fit for animals. ~ DEN
D17. A propeller. ~ OAR
D18. Nickname of a late president. ~ TED
D19. This goes around on a petting party. ~ ARM
D21. Someone often made to walk the plank. ~ NOMINEE
D23. These are just scraps no one should want. ~ BATTLES
D25. This has no feet but it runs. ~ WATER
D27. This fellow sure digs for a living. ~ MINER
D28. Lots of people chew this. ~ RAG
D30. This little fellow was caught in the jam; but not on the street. ~ TOT
D31. This is often put on a horse. ~ BET
D32. An asinine animal. ~ ASS
D36. This is no good on earth. ~ BALLOON
D38. An expression of terror. ~ SCREAM
D39. This is often—used by the poet. ~ OFT
D40. This is hot stuff. ~ SOL
D41. A beverage (not a kick in a carload). ~ TEA
D43. This goes with a dash. ~ DOT
D44. These often get in the soup. ~ SPOONS
D46. Another kickless beverage. ~ POP
D47. You may not believe in signs but you'll have to stand for this one. ~ SRO
D49. This gets a lot of hard knocks. ~ DOOR
D50. An opening—it gets the coin. ~ SLOT
D55. When you're here—you'll never be found out. ~ IN
D57. This fellow would like a trip to Washington. ~ AL


